## 1. What is  a median? A mean?

**Median:** The middle value of a dataset when the values are arranged in order.

**Mean:** It is the average of all the values, calculated by adding them together and dividing by the number of values.

**Reference:** <https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e>

## 2. What is a variance? A standard deviation?

**Variance:** A measure of how the values spread out around the mean.

**Standard deviation:** The square root of the variance. It shows the typical spread of the data around the mean.

**Reference:** <https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e>

## 3. What is a normal distribution?

A **normal distribution** is a symmetric, bell-shaped distribution where most values are close to the mean.

**Reference:** <https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/data-science/mathematics-probability-distributions-set-3-normal-distribution>

## 4. What is a statistical test?

A **statistical test** is a method applied to research data to determine the importance of results and assess if those results occurred by chance or not, allowing researchers to draw conclusions.

**Reference:** <https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/statistical-test>


## 5. When to use a statistical test?

We use a statistical test when we want to determine whether an observed result is likely to be due to a real effect or to random variation or by chance.

**Reference:** <https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/statistical-test>


## 6. What is a parametric test? A non-parametric test?

**Parametric test:** A test that makes assumptions about the distribution of the data, such as normal distribution.

**Non-parametric test:** A test that makes fewer assumptions and uses medians and rankings for the data.

**Reference:** <https://builtin.com/data-science/parametric-vs-nonparametric>


## 7. What are the assumptions of parametric tests?

Common assumptions are:

1. Normality – Data in each group should be normally distributed.

2. Equal Variance – Data in each group should have approximately equal variance.

3. Independence – Data in each group should be randomly and independently sampled from the population.

4. No Outliers – There should be no extreme outliers.

**Reference:** <https://www.statology.org/parametric-tests-assumptions>

## 8. What are the assumptions of non-parametric tests?

Non-parametric tests generally require fewer assumptions, but the observations still need to be independent, randomly sampled (collected appropriately) and the data must be suitable for the chosen test. 

**Reference:** <https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section2/prc2.htm>

## 9. What is a p-value?

A **p-value** tells us how likely the observed result is if there is no real effect.

A small p-value suggests that the result is less likely to be due to chance.

**Reference:** <https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/9-4-rare-events-the-sample-decision-and-conclusion>


## 10. What is the risk of error when using a statistical test?

There are two main errors:

- **Type I error:** Rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true.
- **Type II error:** Not rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually false.

**Reference:** <https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/9-2-outcomes-and-the-type-i-and-type-ii-errors>

## 11. What is the difference between a paired and an unpaired test?

**Paired test:** Used when the observations are related, such as measurements taken from the same person before and after an intervention.

**Unpaired test:** Used when the observations come from two independent groups.

**Reference:** <https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881615/>